### II-A Tasks

In the perception module of autonomous vehicles, semantic segmentation, object detection, object localization, and classification/recognition constitute the foundation for reliable navigation and accurate decision [[38](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib38)]. These tasks are described as follows respectively:

* • 3D point cloud semantic segmentation: Point cloud segmentation is the process to cluster the input data into several homogeneous regions, where points in the same region have the identical attributes [[39](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib39)]. Each input point is predicted with a semantic label, such as ground, tree, building. The task can be concluded as: given a set of ordered 3D points X={x1,x2,xi,⋯,xn}𝑋subscript𝑥1subscript𝑥2subscript𝑥𝑖⋯subscript𝑥𝑛X=\left\{x\_{1},x\_{2},x\_{i},\cdots,x\_{n}\right\} with xi∈R3subscript𝑥𝑖superscript𝑅3{x\_{i}}\in{R^{3}} and a candidate label set Y={y1,y2,⋯,yk}𝑌subscript𝑦1subscript𝑦2⋯subscript𝑦𝑘Y=\left\{y\_{1},y\_{2},\cdots,{y\_{k}}\right\}, assign each input point xisubscript𝑥𝑖x\_{i} with one of the k semantic labels [[40](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib40)]. Segmentation results can further support object detection and classification, as shown in Fig.[2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ II-B Challenges and Problems ‣ II Tasks and Challenges ‣ Deep Learning for LiDAR Point Clouds in Autonomous Driving: A Review")(a). * • 3D object detection/localization: Given an arbitrary point cloud data, the goal of 3D object detection is to detect and locate the instances of predefined categories (e.g., cars, pedestrians, and cyclists, as shown in Fig.[2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ II-B Challenges and Problems ‣ II Tasks and Challenges ‣ Deep Learning for LiDAR Point Clouds in Autonomous Driving: A Review")(b)), and return their geometric 3D location, orientation and semantic instance label [[41](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib41)]. Such information can be represented coarsely using a 3D bounding box which is tightly bounding the detected object [[42](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib42), [42](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib42), [13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib13)]. This box is commonly represented as (x,y,z,h,w,l,θ,c)𝑥𝑦𝑧ℎ𝑤𝑙𝜃𝑐(x,y,z,h,w,l,\theta,c), where (x,y,z)𝑥𝑦𝑧(x,y,z) denotes the object (bounding box) center position, (h,w,l)ℎ𝑤𝑙(h,w,l) represents the bounding box size with width, length and height, and θ𝜃\theta is the object orientation. The orientation refers to the rigid transformation that aligns the detected object to its instance in the scene, which are the translations in each of the of x, y, and z directions as well as a rotation about each of these three axes [[43](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib43), [44](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib44)]. c𝑐c represents the semantic label of this bounding box (object). * • 3D object classification/recognition: Given several groups of point clouds, the objectiveness of classification /recognition is to determine the category (e.g., mug, table, or car, as shown in Fig.[2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ II-B Challenges and Problems ‣ II Tasks and Challenges ‣ Deep Learning for LiDAR Point Clouds in Autonomous Driving: A Review")(c)) the group points belong to. The problem of 3D object classification can be defined as: given a set of 3D ordered points X={x1,x2,xi,⋯,xn}𝑋subscript𝑥1subscript𝑥2subscript𝑥𝑖⋯subscript𝑥𝑛X=\left\{x\_{1},x\_{2},x\_{i},\cdots,x\_{n}\right\} with xi∈R3subscript𝑥𝑖superscript𝑅3x\_{i}\in{R^{3}} and a candidate label set Y={y1,y2,⋯,yk}𝑌subscript𝑦1subscript𝑦2⋯subscript𝑦𝑘Y=\left\{y\_{1},y\_{2},\cdots,{y\_{k}}\right\}, assign the whole point set X𝑋X with one of the k𝑘k labels [[45](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib45)].

3D point cloud semantic segmentation: Point cloud segmentation is the process to cluster the input data into several homogeneous regions, where points in the same region have the identical attributes [[39](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib39)]. Each input point is predicted with a semantic label, such as ground, tree, building. The task can be concluded as: given a set of ordered 3D points X={x1,x2,xi,⋯,xn}𝑋subscript𝑥1subscript𝑥2subscript𝑥𝑖⋯subscript𝑥𝑛X=\left\{x\_{1},x\_{2},x\_{i},\cdots,x\_{n}\right\} with xi∈R3subscript𝑥𝑖superscript𝑅3{x\_{i}}\in{R^{3}} and a candidate label set Y={y1,y2,⋯,yk}𝑌subscript𝑦1subscript𝑦2⋯subscript𝑦𝑘Y=\left\{y\_{1},y\_{2},\cdots,{y\_{k}}\right\}, assign each input point xisubscript𝑥𝑖x\_{i} with one of the k semantic labels [[40](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#bib.bib40)]. Segmentation results can further support object detection and classification, as shown in Fig.[2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2005.09830#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ II-B Challenges and Problems ‣ II Tasks and Challenges ‣ Deep Learning for LiDAR Point Clouds in Autonomous Driving: A Review")(a).

